      Why Government Portals Reject Uploaded Documents (And How to Fix It)
    </h2>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-base">
      <p>
        Every year, tens of thousands of competitive exam candidates face application rejection or penalty fees due to document upload failures. Government server engines run automated server-side verification scripts with zero tolerance for specification errors. Understanding why these rejections happen is the key to passing document screening:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li>
          <strong>The Strict Byte-Limit Ceiling:</strong> Most government servers (such as NIC, TCS iON, and CDAC portals) evaluate file sizes in exact bytes, where 1 KB equals exactly 1,024 bytes. A 20 KB limit equals 20,480 bytes. If a standard compressor exports a file at 20.1 KB (20,582 bytes), the portal parser automatically aborts the form submission with an error: <em>"File size must not exceed 20 KB"</em>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Floating-Point Metadata Overflow:</strong> Many traditional image tools leave EXIF metadata, camera orientation markers, GPS coordinates, and color profiles embedded inside the JPEG header. This hidden metadata can add 2 KB to 6 KB of invisible bloat, pushing an ostensibly small image well past portal thresholds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The Slider Guesswork Trap:</strong> Generic online photo editors provide a non-deterministic quality slider (e.g., 70% or 80%). Moving the slider down by 1% can unexpectedly swing file size by several kilobytes depending on image complexity and noise levels, forcing users into frustrating trial-and-error cycles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The SetImgKB Safety Buffer Solution:</strong> To eliminate portal rejections permanently, SetImgKB's mathematical engine targets a strict <strong>[target × 0.95, target × 0.98]</strong> corridor. When you select a 20 KB target, the engine guarantees an output strictly between <strong>19.0 KB and 19.6 KB</strong> (19,456 to 20,070 bytes). It strips unnecessary EXIF headers while preserving pixel density, guaranteeing 100% portal acceptance.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
